WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.180
Begin with the nephron overview.

00:00:03.020 --> 00:00:08.660
A is the renal corpuscle, B the proximal convoluted tubule, C the descending

00:00:08.660 --> 00:00:11.620
limb and D the ascending limb of the nephron loop.

00:00:12.340 --> 00:00:16.500
E is the distal convoluted tubule, leading toward the collecting duct at F.

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:22.180
Follow the arrows down C, around the hairpin and up D before reaching E.

00:00:22.820 --> 00:00:26.420
The collecting duct receives fluid from more than one nephron and is included

00:00:26.420 --> 00:00:27.160
for continuity.

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:31.840
The dashed line separates cortex from medulla in this simplified view.

00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:36.480
Loop depth varies among nephron types, and this drawing is not a scale

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:37.400
measurement of a kidney.

00:00:37.968 --> 00:00:42.628
The overview shows a fluid route, not a claim that everything entering it must

00:00:42.628 --> 00:00:43.408
leave as urine.

00:00:44.268 --> 00:00:47.868
Reabsorption returns material from tubular fluid toward the circulation;

00:00:48.828 --> 00:00:52.906
secretion adds selected material from the blood or interstitial side to

00:00:52.906 --> 00:00:53.308
tubular

00:00:53.308 --> 00:00:53.648
fluid.

00:00:54.248 --> 00:00:58.806
The proximal tubule at B is an important site of recovery of filtered water

00:00:58.806 --> 00:00:58.988
and

00:00:58.988 --> 00:00:59.308
solutes.

00:00:59.988 --> 00:01:04.468
In the standard loop comparison, the descending limb at C permits water

00:01:04.468 --> 00:01:09.608
movement, whereas the ascending limb at D reabsorbs salts and is relatively

00:01:09.608 --> 00:01:10.808
impermeable to water.

00:01:11.508 --> 00:01:14.028
These differences contribute to concentrating mechanisms.

00:01:14.808 --> 00:01:18.928
Do not swap blood flow arrows with tubular flow arrows or interpret filtered

00:01:18.928 --> 00:01:21.728
glucose as automatically excreted glucose.

00:01:22.128 --> 00:01:24.728
Switch to the renal corpuscle close up.

00:01:25.468 --> 00:01:30.568
The solid arrows identify blood flow: afferent arteriole A enters the

00:01:30.568 --> 00:01:35.668
glomerular capillary tuft B, and efferent arteriole D carries blood away.

00:01:36.208 --> 00:01:40.088
The dashed arrows show a different route: filtration from the tuft toward

00:01:40.088 --> 00:01:44.468
capsular space C and onward toward the proximal tubule at E.

00:01:44.888 --> 00:01:47.088
E is not the efferent arteriole.

00:01:47.088 --> 00:01:51.628
Two exit routes appear because blood and filtrate are different compartments.

00:01:52.328 --> 00:01:56.868
The sketch cuts the capsule open schematically at its vascular and tubular

00:01:56.868 --> 00:02:00.328
poles; it does not show every layer of the filtration barrier.

00:02:01.208 --> 00:02:03.128
Read the arrow key before naming an outlet.

00:02:03.480 --> 00:02:08.160
The normal glomerular filtration barrier retains blood cells and most large

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:12.440
plasma proteins while allowing water and many small solutes to enter the

00:02:12.440 --> 00:02:13.260
capsular space.

00:02:14.380 --> 00:02:17.920
Filtrate therefore contains useful substances as well as waste products.

00:02:18.740 --> 00:02:21.920
The subsequent tubules recover much of the useful material.

00:02:22.720 --> 00:02:26.707
Do not describe the glomerulus as a sieve that selectively removes only

00:02:26.707 --> 00:02:27.100
wastes.

00:02:28.120 --> 00:02:31.770
Likewise, the size of a drawn tuft does not provide a measured filtration

00:02:31.770 --> 00:02:32.020
rate.

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:35.620
Calculating a rate requires data beyond this schematic.

00:02:36.760 --> 00:02:40.540
Keep the normal barrier, the composition of the initial filtrate and later

00:02:40.540 --> 00:02:43.580
tubular processing as three distinct parts of the explanation.

00:02:43.992 --> 00:02:47.712
Now identify a typical myelinated multipolar neuron.

00:02:48.652 --> 00:02:53.325
A points to branching dendrites and B to the cell body, or soma, containing

00:02:53.325 --> 00:02:53.512
the

00:02:53.512 --> 00:02:53.832
nucleus.

00:02:54.372 --> 00:02:57.052
C identifies the axon near its origin.

00:02:58.032 --> 00:03:03.792
D marks a myelin segment, E a node of Ranvier between segments, and F the

00:03:03.792 --> 00:03:04.832
branching axon terminals.

00:03:05.372 --> 00:03:08.812
The lower arrow indicates the usual functional route toward the terminals.

00:03:09.552 --> 00:03:13.452
Do not identify dendrites only by the left side of a page: another figure can

00:03:13.452 --> 00:03:14.012
be rotated.

00:03:14.932 --> 00:03:18.432
Neuron shapes vary, so this schematic is not a universal shape for all

00:03:18.432 --> 00:03:18.832
sensory,

00:03:19.232 --> 00:03:20.932
motor and interneuron classes.

00:03:21.632 --> 00:03:24.512
Use branching, continuity and the label endpoints together.

00:03:25.152 --> 00:03:28.372
Keep the neuron overview and compare D with E.

00:03:29.312 --> 00:03:33.732
Myelin around the axon at D acts as insulation that reduces current leakage.

00:03:34.412 --> 00:03:38.592
In the standard myelinated axon account, local electrical current spreads

00:03:38.592 --> 00:03:43.366
between nodes and action potentials are regenerated at nodal regions such as

00:03:43.366 --> 00:03:43.492
E.

00:03:43.972 --> 00:03:45.952
This is called saltatory conduction.

00:03:46.672 --> 00:03:50.432
The signal does not mean that one sodium ion physically jumps from the cell

00:03:50.432 --> 00:03:55.132
body to every terminal, and it is not a transmitter filled vesicle travelling

00:03:55.132 --> 00:03:56.412
as the action potential.

00:03:57.472 --> 00:04:00.092
Myelin thickness and node spacing are not drawn to scale.

00:04:01.052 --> 00:04:05.412
Identify the sheath, axon and node separately before connecting each to its

00:04:05.412 --> 00:04:05.732
function.

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:09.540
The synapse close up enlarges a typical chemical junction.

00:04:10.300 --> 00:04:13.040
A identifies a vesicle inside the presynaptic terminal.

00:04:13.760 --> 00:04:19.200
B points to the presynaptic membrane, C the synaptic cleft, and D receptors at

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:20.340
the postsynaptic membrane.

00:04:21.220 --> 00:04:25.300
The small dots represent released transmitter molecules, not whole vesicles

00:04:25.300 --> 00:04:26.320
crossing the gap.

00:04:26.780 --> 00:04:30.500
A transmitter must reach suitable receptors to influence the receiving cell.

00:04:30.880 --> 00:04:35.280
The membranes remain distinct across the cleft; they are not drawn as one

00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:36.320
continuous axon.

00:04:37.140 --> 00:04:39.720
Read the upper and lower cell labels as well as the arrows.

00:04:40.460 --> 00:04:44.166
This figure shows a chemical synapse and should not be used as a complete

00:04:44.166 --> 00:04:44.420
model

00:04:44.420 --> 00:04:45.580
of an electrical synapse.

00:04:46.656 --> 00:04:50.936
In the typical chemical synapse account, arrival of an action potential at the

00:04:50.936 --> 00:04:55.896
presynaptic terminal leads to calcium entry and transmitter release by vesicle

00:04:55.896 --> 00:04:56.256
fusion.

00:04:57.416 --> 00:05:01.896
Released molecules cross the cleft at C and act on appropriate receptors at D.

00:05:02.616 --> 00:05:07.276
The resulting response can be excitatory or inhibitory, depending on the

00:05:07.276 --> 00:05:11.276
receptor and cellular context, and does not guarantee a new action potential

00:05:11.276 --> 00:05:12.756
after every release event.

00:05:13.516 --> 00:05:17.116
Direction in this diagram follows the asymmetric arrangement of release

00:05:17.116 --> 00:05:18.376
machinery and receptors.

00:05:19.296 --> 00:05:22.636
Do not describe the gap as a place where an intact action potential simply

00:05:22.636 --> 00:05:23.836
leaps through open space.

00:05:24.836 --> 00:05:28.296
Electrical synapses use a different coupling mechanism that is not pictured

00:05:28.296 --> 00:05:28.656
here.
